{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, current stable release, freeze 2021-07-28). Ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins, and manganese catalases are three separate families inside one superfamily: Ferritin (a.25.1.1, sunid 47241), Ribonucleotide reductase-like (a.25.1.2, sunid 47253), and Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (a.25.1.3, sunid 100951). All three are children of the Ferritin-like superfamily a.25.1 (sunid 47240), which sits in the Ferritin-like fold a.25 (sunid 47239) in class a (all-alpha, sunid 46456). The fold comment reads 'core: 4 helices; bundle, closed, left-handed twist; 1 crossover connection'. The superfamily comment reads 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. The superfamily node's homology section reports homology_implied_by_level = true (scope 'sf') with no warnings and no heterogeneity flags; the fold node reports homology_implied_by_level = false with the warning 'SCOPe levels above superfamily do not imply homology'. Deterministic pairwise comparisons of one representative domain per family in the same release (horse L-chain apoferritin d2za7a_, a.25.1.1; E. coli ribonucleotide reductase R2 d1mxra_, a.25.1.2; Lactobacillus plantarum Mn catalase d1o9ia_, a.25.1.3) all return relationship 'same_superfamily' with lowest shared node a.25.1 and no caveats. The a.25.1.2 family also contains stearoyl-ACP desaturase, methane monooxygenase alpha/beta subunits, toluene/o-xylene monooxygenase subunits and PaaC, and a.25.1 holds additional families (YciF-like, half-ferritin, PMT1231-like, MiaE-like, AMB4284-like, Rv2844-like, automated matches a.25.1.0). The fold a.25 also contains other superfamilies (a.25.2 cobalamin adenosyltransferase-like, a.25.3 EsxAB dimer-like, a.25.4 PE/PPE dimer-like, a.25.5, a.25.6) that share only the four-helix bundle topology with no SCOPe homology assertion.

Interpretation. The correct reading is divergent evolution from a common ancestor with functional divergence, not convergence. The three groups share a superfamily but not a family, so SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry at the superfamily level (the homology-bearing level) while placing them in different families because sequence similarity is low and functions differ. What unites them in SCOPe is the shared four-helix-bundle architecture plus the conserved di-metal (bimetal) centre in the middle of the bundle; the curators treated this shared, positionally conserved metal site as the structural/functional evidence of common origin. The different chemistries (iron storage/ferroxidase activity in ferritins, tyrosyl-radical generation and O2 activation by a diiron site in RNR R2 and the related diiron oxygenases/desaturases, and H2O2 disproportionation by a dimanganese site in Mn catalases) are an example of a conserved metal-binding scaffold being recruited to different reactions. SCOPe records this as 'same superfamily, different family', which is the appropriate evolutionary strength: stronger than fold-level similarity (which alone would not imply homology, as the a.25.2-a.25.6 superfamilies show), weaker than family-level relationship.

Limits. (1) The superfamily placement does not license function transfer: the identity of the metal (Fe vs Mn), the reaction, substrate, oligomeric state (e.g. 24-mer ferritin cage vs dimeric R2 vs hexameric Mn catalase) and physiological role cannot be inferred from a.25.1 membership; the specific chemistries stated above are pretrained biological knowledge, not SCOPe data, and SCOPe only annotates 'bimetal-ion centre' generically. (2) Members of different families here are poor interchangeable structural templates for each other's active-site details; superfamily-level relationships typically preserve core topology but not metal-ligand identity, second-shell residues, or loop geometry, so modelling one from another requires an inspected alignment. (3) SCOPe does not expose the rationale (alignments, metal-ligand correspondence, literature) behind the superfamily merge; confirming the hypothesis of a shared ancestral di-metal site requires external evidence such as a structure-based alignment showing equivalent metal-ligand positions (the Glu/His ligand motifs on helices B and D), sequence profile/HMM evidence linking the families, and phylogenetic analysis. (4) Representative selection caveats: d2za7a_ is an automated match to d1data_ and a mutant; d1o9ia_ is a mutant; a.25.1.0 and the per-family 'automated matches' protein nodes are automated assignments, so any benchmark or template use should choose curated, non-mutant representatives and check SPACI/AEROSPACI quality records (not retrieved here). (5) The conclusion is release-specific to SCOPe 2.08; historical changes (e.g. when the Mn catalase family was added) were not queried and are not asserted. (6) Same fold but different superfamily (e.g. a.25.2 cobalamin adenosyltransferase-like) means SCOPe makes no homology assertion, not that those proteins are proven analogs.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__